# Importing a GIF as a segment.
#
# A GIF is the awkward source on both counts a source can be awkward: it carries no audio, and
# its rate is neither the project's nor a whole number. ffprobe reports 12.5 fps for one written
# at 12 in a 1/100 time base, so a clip of it lands on a fractional number of timeline frames.
#
# That arithmetic was already right in TimelineEdits.ImportSource and in TimelineResolver, and
# already wrong in ProjectInvariants, which compared a length in timeline frames against a count
# of source frames — so every mixed-rate append was rejected as reading past the end of its own
# source. A 15 fps clip failed exactly the same way; the GIF is only what made it obvious.

sample base.mp4 4
open base.mp4
assert-has-media
assert-segments 1

# 4 seconds at 30 fps.
state

sample-gif clip.gif 3
append clip.gif
assert-segments 2

# 36 frames at 12.5 fps is 2.88s, which is 86.4 frames of a 30 fps timeline — floored to 86, so
# the clip never claims a frame it does not have. 120 + 86 = 206.
assert-frame 120
state

# The picture, not just the document: testsrc2 prints its own clock into the frame, so a capture
# inside the GIF's span says whether the rate conversion put us where we asked to be.
goto 150
dump-preview 01-inside-the-gif.png
shot 02-timeline

# It undoes as one step, like any other append.
key Ctrl+Z
assert-segments 1
state
